WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.953
Large one-shot requests are difficult to verify.

2
00:00:02.953 --> 00:00:08.439
If a single prompt changes the user interface, database, service layer, authorization rules,

3
00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:13.924
and deployment configuration, the resulting patch may be impressive but hard to understand.

4
00:00:13.924 --> 00:00:18.987
When something fails, you may not know which part introduced the problem,

5
00:00:18.987 --> 00:00:22.784
and useful work can be mixed with unrelated damage.

6
00:00:22.784 --> 00:00:27.847
Decomposition turns a broad goal into stages that produce visible intermediate results.

7
00:00:27.847 --> 00:00:35.442
A feature might begin by confirming current behavior, then adding or updating tests, then changing the data model,

8
00:00:35.442 --> 00:00:40.505
then implementing service logic, then connecting the interface, and finally running validation.

9
00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:45.568
Each stage has a narrow purpose and a clear reason for existing.

10
00:00:45.568 --> 00:00:47.678
Small steps also protect scope.

11
00:00:47.678 --> 00:00:53.163
A feature change should not quietly include broad formatting, dependency upgrades, directory reorganizations,

12
00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:57.382
or unrelated refactoring unless those edits are necessary and approved.

13
00:00:57.382 --> 00:01:04.133
Before implementation, it is often useful to ask for a short plan or a file list.

14
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:12.571
That preview can show whether the assistant intends to edit the wrong layer or touch more files than the task

15
00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:12.993
justifies.

16
00:01:12.993 --> 00:01:21.010
The plan is not bureaucracy; it is an inexpensive way to catch drift before it becomes a sprawling diff.

17
00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.385
Checkpoints turn the conversation into engineering control points.

18
00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:29.027
After a meaningful step, inspect the diff, run the relevant tests,

19
00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:32.824
and confirm that the project still behaves as expected.

20
00:01:32.824 --> 00:01:34.934
Version control makes this practical.

21
00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:42.950
A clean starting state, a branch for experimentation, and descriptive commits give you a way to preserve good work,

22
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.904
compare alternatives, or revert a bad path.

23
00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:50.967
Small steps are not simply a slower version of a large request.

24
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:53.076
They often reduce total rework.

25
00:01:53.076 --> 00:02:00.671
Failures are easier to isolate, feedback is more precise, and successful intermediate changes can be kept even when

26
00:02:00.671 --> 00:02:02.781
the next idea needs revision.

27
00:02:02.781 --> 00:02:09.953
The assistant can still move quickly, but the developer keeps the change understandable and the project remains

28
00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:12.485
easier for another reviewer to follow.
